  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low and cold front approach the region Sunday \ninto Monday, with rain showers likely across the region.\n\nHigh pressure shifts away from the region late tonight as a low\norganizes and develops over the Midwest. The warm front\nassociated with this low may briefly lift into the region on\nSunday, but will be quickly followed by a cold front Sunday\nNight into Monday Morning. \n\nRegardless of the exact evolution of the warm front, should\nstart to see rain showers move into the region by late this\nevening in response to modest mid and upper level synoptic scale\nlift associate with the cyclogensis. \n\nAs far as hazards, there remains a concern for localized flash\nflooding, particularly in urban areas, however that threat has\ndecreased slightly. At the surface, a persistent easterly flow \nwill be in place through tomorrow, gradually becoming \nsoutheasterly. As such, low level moisture will be on the \nincrease, with surface dew points rising well into the 60s and \nprecipitable water values increasing to near 2 inches.\nPrecipitable water values that high are well above the 90th\npercentile. \n\nMost models have trended faster with the low and then front \nsweeping through the region. This is the main factor why \nforecasted rain amounts now range from 0.1 to 1.5 inches from \nTonight through Monday. Additionally, the mid and upper level\nsynoptic scale lift never really line up with low level\nfrontogenesis, thus limiting lift.\n\nThe risk for excessive rainfall is a marginal, or 1 out of 4, \nthrough this period. However, with abundant moisture, locally \nheavy rain will still be possible, and if it occurs over urban \nand other poor drainage areas, localized flash flooding is \npossible. Additionally, if the warm front is able to lift into \nour region, areas along and south of the warm front could have \n\nThe risk for other thunderstorm hazards is low due to very\nlimited instability.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stationary front south of the region could \nkeep rain chances around, especially for Delmarva through mid \nweek.\n\nOnce the cold front sweeps through on Monday, it is expected to\nstall south of our region. How far south that front stalls will\nbe a big factor in how much rain we see through the week. The\nfurther south it stalls, the less likely our region will see\ncontinued rain chances through mid week. Regardless, our region\nshould generally be on the cool side of the front through this\nperiod keeping temperatures below normal and low level moisture\nlower than what it is expected to be on Sunday. \n\nAn upper level low digging in from the northwest could spark \nanother round of cyclogensis by Wednesday, which could result in\nthe stationary front sliding north as a warm front briefly,\nthough it will still likely stay south of our region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A closed low could develop off our coast by \nThursday, potentially bringing another chance for rain, and \nincreasing the risk for coastal flooding.\n\nAs early as Tuesday, there looks to be an inverted surface\ntrough off the coast of the Carolinas. However, most models\ndon't show this low closing off until Wednesday or Thursday.\nThat is not the only way the models differ; there are\nsignificant differences in the track of the low, ranging from\nthe GFS showing the center of the low as close as 120 miles from\nour coast, to the ICON, with the center of the low getting no\ncloser than 500 miles from our coast! \n\nThe closer the track of this low is to the coast, the more\nlikely our region is to see rain, and gusty winds in the\nThursday and Friday time period. \n\nIt also bears watching as it could increase the risk for coastal\nflooding. The full moon is on Saturday, Sept 26, so persistent\nonshore flow Thursday and Friday will only increase the risk as\nastronomical tides increase ahead of the full moon.",
